# Part 6 — Common Python Exceptions
#
# 1. ValueError
#    int("hello") tries to convert words into a number, which Python cannot do.
#
# 2. ZeroDivisionError
#    10 % 0 tries to divide by zero, which is mathematically impossible.
#
# 3. TypeError
#    values[1.5] tries to use a decimal number as a list position, but list positions must be whole numbers.
#
# 4. AttributeError
#    values.depend(3) tries to call depend(), but Python lists do not have a built in depend action.

#Part 7 — The Default except Branch
# Purpose of the final except branch:
# to catch runtime errors.
#
# When it executes:
# It executes only when an exception occurs in the try that does not match ValueError or ZeroDivisionError.
#
# Why it must appear last:
# Python checks except branches in order from top to bottom. If a default except branch comes first, it intercepts every error and prevents the next except branches from ever running.
#
# Why specific handlers are more informative:
# Specific handlers give clear targeted error messages.

